neutron-zzzzzzzzzi-winewayland-sync-lock-state

winewayland: sync Wine's toggle-key state with the compositor's locked modifiers.

SYMPTOM (user, during a live editing session): Caps Lock in Premiere was INVERTED relative to
the rest of the desktop -- typing came out in the wrong case, and stayed wrong for the life of
the process. Toggling Caps Lock inside the app could not fix it, because that flips both
states at once and preserves the inversion.

CAUSE. winewayland tracked Caps Lock and Num Lock only by watching key events, and it receives
key events only while a Wine window holds keyboard focus. Toggle Caps Lock with anything else
focused -- a browser, a terminal, the desktop -- and the two states diverge permanently.
keyboard_handle_modifiers() received mods_locked from the compositor on every modifier change
and threw it away, under a standing upstream FIXME:

    set_current_xkb_group(xkb_group);

    /* FIXME: Sync wine modifier state with XKB modifier state. */

FIX: compare Wine's toggle bit against xkb's XKB_STATE_MODS_LOCKED for Caps and Num, and when
they disagree, send the key so Wine's own machinery flips its state. This is deliberately the
same shape winex11.drv has used for years (update_lock_state / adjust_lock_state in its
keyboard.c), including leading with the RELEASE when the key is physically held, so a
synthetic press is never fabricated on top of a real one.

Hooked into the modifiers handler rather than wl_keyboard.enter on purpose: the compositor
always sends a modifiers event immediately after enter, so one site covers both focus changes
and mid-session toggles, and there is no second code path to keep in sync.

Num Lock had the identical defect and is fixed by the same loop; the user had simply not hit
it yet.

Verified live: toggle Caps Lock with another window focused, return to Premiere -- state now
matches the desktop, in both directions. No stray toggles observed while typing (the sync
fires only when the two states actually disagree).

Upstream-worthy: this closes the FIXME rather than working around it, and affects every
winewayland user of every application.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
@@ -31,6 +31,8 @@
 #include <sys/mman.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 
+#include <xkbcommon/xkbcommon-names.h>
+
 #include "waylanddrv.h"
 #include "wine/debug.h"
 #include "wine/server.h"
@@ -895,16 +897,79 @@
     NtUserSendHardwareInput(hwnd, 0, &input, 0);
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: sync Wine's toggle-key state with the compositor's LOCKED modifiers.
+ *
+ * winewayland tracked Caps Lock and Num Lock only by watching the key events it receives --
+ * and it receives them only while a Wine window holds keyboard focus. Toggle Caps Lock with
+ * anything else focused and Wine stays inverted relative to the desktop, which the user hit
+ * in Premiere: typing came out in the wrong case until the app was restarted. Toggling it
+ * again inside the app cannot help, because that flips both states at once.
+ *
+ * This is the FIXME that stood immediately below: "Sync wine modifier state with XKB modifier
+ * state." winex11.drv has done it for years (update_lock_state / adjust_lock_state in its
+ * keyboard.c) and this is deliberately the same shape: compare Wine's toggle bit against the
+ * server's locked state and, when they disagree, send the key so Wine's own machinery flips
+ * it. Doing it here also covers focus changes, because the compositor always sends a
+ * modifiers event straight after wl_keyboard.enter. */
+static void wayland_sync_lock_state(HWND hwnd)
+{
+    static const struct
+    {
+        const char *xkb_name;
+        WORD vkey;
+        WORD scan;
+        DWORD flags;
+    }
+    locks[] =
+    {
+        { XKB_MOD_NAME_CAPS, VK_CAPITAL, 0x3a, 0 },
+        { XKB_MOD_NAME_NUM,  VK_NUMLOCK, 0x45, KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY },
+    };
+    struct wayland_keyboard *keyboard = &process_wayland.keyboard;
+    BYTE keystate[256];
+    unsigned int i;
+
+    if (!NtUserGetAsyncKeyboardState(keystate)) return;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(locks); ++i)
+    {
+        INPUT input = {0};
+        BOOL locked;
+
+        pthread_mutex_lock(&keyboard->mutex);
+        locked = keyboard->xkb_state && xkb_state_mod_name_is_active(keyboard->xkb_state,
+                locks[i].xkb_name, XKB_STATE_MODS_LOCKED) > 0;
+        pthread_mutex_unlock(&keyboard->mutex);
+
+        if (!(keystate[locks[i].vkey] & 0x01) == !locked) continue;
+
+        TRACE("adjusting %s: wine=%d compositor=%d\n", locks[i].xkb_name,
+              !!(keystate[locks[i].vkey] & 0x01), locked);
+
+        input.type = INPUT_KEYBOARD;
+        input.ki.wVk = locks[i].vkey;
+        input.ki.wScan = locks[i].scan;
+        /* If the key is physically held right now, lead with the release -- the ordering
+         * winex11 uses, so we never fabricate a press on top of a real one. */
+        input.ki.dwFlags = locks[i].flags;
+        if (keystate[locks[i].vkey] & 0x80) input.ki.dwFlags |= KEYEVENTF_KEYUP;
+        NtUserSendHardwareInput(hwnd, 0, &input, 0);
+        input.ki.dwFlags ^= KEYEVENTF_KEYUP;
+        NtUserSendHardwareInput(hwnd, 0, &input, 0);
+    }
+}
+
 static void keyboard_handle_modifiers(void *data, struct wl_keyboard *wl_keyboard,
                                       uint32_t serial, uint32_t mods_depressed,
                                       uint32_t mods_latched, uint32_t mods_locked,
                                       uint32_t xkb_group)
 {
     struct wayland_keyboard *keyboard = &process_wayland.keyboard;
+    HWND hwnd;
 
     InterlockedExchange(&process_wayland.input_serial, serial);
 
-    if (!wayland_keyboard_get_focused_hwnd()) return;
+    if (!(hwnd = wayland_keyboard_get_focused_hwnd())) return;
 
     TRACE("serial=%u mods_depressed=%#x mods_latched=%#x mods_locked=%#x xkb_group=%d stub!\n",
           serial, mods_depressed, mods_latched, mods_locked, xkb_group);
@@ -916,7 +981,7 @@
 
     set_current_xkb_group(xkb_group);
 
-    /* FIXME: Sync wine modifier state with XKB modifier state. */
+    wayland_sync_lock_state(hwnd);
 }
 
 static void keyboard_handle_repeat_info(void *data, struct wl_keyboard *wl_keyboard,
